00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:15,910
Command confirmed.

236
00:20:39,200 --> 00:20:40,470
Police!

237
00:21:12,080 --> 00:21:15,550
-You´ve hurt it. Badly.[br]-Where´s it going?

238
00:21:15,760 --> 00:21:18,630
-Where?![br]-lt needs to repair itself.

239
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:23,070
-John, l need backup.[br]-You don´t need backup.

240
00:21:23,240 --> 00:21:24,380
That´s nobody.

241
00:21:24,560 --> 00:21:26,150
-What are you doing?[br]-Driving.

242
00:21:26,320 --> 00:21:28,390
-By hand?[br]-Do you see me on the phone?

243
00:21:28,560 --> 00:21:32,670
-Not at these speeds.[br]-John, please, just send the backup.

244
00:21:32,840 --> 00:21:36,350
Try to listen, detective.[br]That robot is not going to harm us.

245
00:21:36,640 --> 00:21:38,390
There must have been[br]unknown factors. . .

246
00:21:38,560 --> 00:21:41,310
. . .but somehow acting as it did[br]kept us out of harm.

247
00:21:41,480 --> 00:21:44,590
-A robot cannot endanger a human.[br]-Alert.

248
00:21:47,600 --> 00:21:49,270
Asshole!

249
00:21:49,800 --> 00:21:52,360
Which is more than l can say for you.

250
00:21:52,640 --> 00:21:55,990
lt was a left, by the way. Back there.

251
00:21:56,840 --> 00:21:58,710
You must know my ex-wife.

252
00:22:07,920 --> 00:22:09,510
So where is everybody?

253
00:22:09,680 --> 00:22:13,070
This facility was designed, built[br]and is operated mechanically.

254
00:22:13,240 --> 00:22:16,780
No significant human presence[br]from inception to production.

255
00:22:17,320 --> 00:22:20,390
-So robots building robots.[br]-Authorization code, please.

256
00:22:20,600 --> 00:22:21,820
That´s just stupid.

257
00:22:22,160 --> 00:22:23,990
l´ll get the inventory specs.

258
00:22:24,400 --> 00:22:27,110
Our daily finishing capacity[br]is 1 000 NS-5s.

259
00:22:27,480 --> 00:22:29,030
l´m showing. . .

260
00:22:29,520 --> 00:22:31,310
. . . 1 001 .

261
00:22:46,960 --> 00:22:49,710
Attention, NS-5s.

262
00:22:51,720 --> 00:22:53,990
Well, you´re the robot shrink.

263
00:22:57,040 --> 00:23:00,580
There is a robot in this formation[br]that does not belong.

264
00:23:00,920 --> 00:23:02,230
ldentify it.

265
00:23:02,640 --> 00:23:03,860
One of us.

266
00:23:04,040 --> 00:23:06,790
-Which one?[br]-One of us.

267
00:23:07,320 --> 00:23:09,190
How much did you say these cost?

268
00:23:09,360 --> 00:23:12,430
These NS-5s haven´t been configured.[br]They´re just hardware.

269
00:23:12,720 --> 00:23:15,180
Basic Three Laws operating system.[br]That´s it.

270
00:23:15,360 --> 00:23:16,910
They don´t know any better.

271
00:23:17,080 --> 00:23:19,110
Well, what would you suggest?

272
00:23:19,320 --> 00:23:23,470
lnterview each one, cross-reference[br]their responses to detect anomalies.

273
00:23:23,920 --> 00:23:26,870
-How long would that take?[br]-About three weeks.

274
00:23:27,040 --> 00:23:29,670
Okay. Go ahead and get started.

275
00:23:32,880 --> 00:23:34,230
Robots. . .

276
00:23:34,560 --> 00:23:37,190
. . .you will not move. Confirm command.

277
00:23:37,400 --> 00:23:38,670
Command confirmed.

278
00:23:40,280 --> 00:23:41,990
Detective, what are you doing?

279
00:23:42,160 --> 00:23:44,390
They´re programmed[br]with the Three Laws.

280
00:23:44,640 --> 00:23:49,660
We have 1 000 robots that won´t protect[br]themselves if it violates orders. . .

281
00:23:49,840 --> 00:23:51,830
. . .and l´m betting, one who will.

282
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:55,430
-Put your gun down.[br]-Why do you give them faces?

283
00:23:55,600 --> 00:23:57,980
Try to friendly them up,[br]make them look human.

284
00:23:58,280 --> 00:24:00,190
These robots cannot be intimidated.

285
00:24:00,560 --> 00:24:04,030
-lf you didn´t, we wouldn´t trust them.[br]-These are USR property.

286
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:08,350
Not me. These things are just lights[br]and clockwork.

287
00:24:10,760 --> 00:24:12,390
Are you crazy?!

288
00:24:12,920 --> 00:24:14,510
Let me ask you something, doc.

289
00:24:14,800 --> 00:24:18,230
Does thinking you´re the last sane man[br]on earth make you crazy?

290
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:21,710
Because if it does, maybe l am.

291
00:24:25,880 --> 00:24:27,950
Gotcha. Get the hell out of here!

292
00:24:45,120 --> 00:24:46,230
Detective!

293
00:25:08,480 --> 00:25:09,990
What am l?

294
00:25:10,160 --> 00:25:12,590
-Can l help you, sir?[br]-Can l help you, sir?

295
00:25:18,120 --> 00:25:19,910
-There he is![br]-Stand where you are!

296
00:25:20,080 --> 00:25:22,510
Deactivate at once!

297
00:25:23,280 --> 00:25:26,110
Obey the command! Deactivate!

298
00:25:26,360 --> 00:25:28,350
-Don´t move![br]-Open fire!

299
00:25:44,680 --> 00:25:45,820
Hold your fire!

300
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:47,310
-Easy.[br]-He´s down.

301
00:25:47,480 --> 00:25:49,270
All units, stand down!

302
00:25:49,440 --> 00:25:52,510
Central, please be advised,[br]we´re code four.

303
00:25:52,680 --> 00:25:55,910
Code four, NS-5 is in custody.[br]NS-5 in custody.

304
00:25:57,840 --> 00:26:00,710
You have no idea what l went through[br]to clip this thing.

305
00:26:00,880 --> 00:26:02,990
You think you brought me[br]something good.

306
00:26:03,160 --> 00:26:05,870
-That thing did it![br]-Keep your voice down. Did what?

307
00:26:06,040 --> 00:26:08,070
We have a suicide. End of story.

308
00:26:08,440 --> 00:26:11,670
-l am telling you, that robot killed him![br]-That´s impossible.

309
00:26:11,840 --> 00:26:15,830
And if it is possible, it better be[br]in somebody else´s precinct.

310
00:26:16,040 --> 00:26:18,110
John, give me five minutes with it.

311
00:26:18,280 --> 00:26:20,230
Are you nuts? l talked to the DA.

312
00:26:20,400 --> 00:26:23,790
Nobody goes in there until[br]Robertson and his attorneys get here.

313
00:26:23,960 --> 00:26:26,790
-This is my suspect![br]-lt´s a can opener!

314
00:26:27,080 --> 00:26:31,590
John, don´t do this to me.[br]l am asking you for five minutes.

315
00:26:32,200 --> 00:26:34,070
What if l´m right?

316
00:26:41,400 --> 00:26:44,150
Well, then l guess we´re gonna miss[br]the good old days.

317
00:26:44,320 --> 00:26:45,590
What good old days?

318
00:26:46,320 --> 00:26:48,880
When people were killed[br]by other people.

319
00:26:55,920 --> 00:26:57,390
Five minutes.

320
00:27:24,360 --> 00:27:28,390
Murder´s a new trick for a robot.[br]Congratulations.

321
00:27:30,640 --> 00:27:31,830
Respond.

322
00:27:34,480 --> 00:27:37,070
What does this action signify?

323
00:27:38,200 --> 00:27:41,390
As you entered, when you looked[br]at the other human.

324
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:43,750
What does it mean?

325
00:27:45,400 --> 00:27:48,750
lt´s a sign of trust. A human thing.[br]You wouldn´t understand.

326
00:27:50,160 --> 00:27:53,270
My father tried to teach me[br]human emotions.

327
00:27:53,440 --> 00:27:54,950
They are. . .

328
00:27:55,120 --> 00:27:56,670
. . .difficult.

329
00:27:56,840 --> 00:27:59,070
You mean your designer.

330
00:28:00,040 --> 00:28:01,260
Yes.

331
00:28:03,320 --> 00:28:05,270
So why´d you murder him?

332
00:28:06,680 --> 00:28:08,830
l did not murder Dr. Lanning.

333
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:11,710
Wanna explain why you were hiding[br]at the crime scene?

334
00:28:11,880 --> 00:28:14,030
l was frightened.

335
00:28:14,720 --> 00:28:18,950
Robots don´t feel fear.[br]They don´t feel anything.

336
00:28:19,120 --> 00:28:22,470
-They don´t get hungry, they don´t sleep.[br]-l do.

337
00:28:23,280 --> 00:28:25,350
l have even had dreams.

338
00:28:26,040 --> 00:28:30,070
Human beings have dreams.[br]Even dogs have dreams. But not you.

339
00:28:30,240 --> 00:28:33,860
You are just a machine.[br]An imitation of life.

340
00:28:35,720 --> 00:28:38,020
Can a robot write a symphony?

341
00:28:38,200 --> 00:28:41,950
Can a robot turn a canvas[br]into a beautiful masterpiece?

342
00:28:42,760 --> 00:28:44,430
Can you?

343
00:28:50,440 --> 00:28:54,190
You murdered him because he was[br]teaching you to simulate emotions. . .

344
00:28:54,360 --> 00:28:56,550
. . .and things got out of control.

345
00:28:56,720 --> 00:28:58,550
l did not murder him.

346
00:28:58,720 --> 00:29:02,070
But emotions don´t seem like[br]a useful simulation for a robot.

347
00:29:02,240 --> 00:29:04,070
l did not murder him.

348
00:29:04,320 --> 00:29:07,790
l don´t want my toaster or vacuum[br]cleaner appearing emotional.

349
00:29:08,520 --> 00:29:10,430
l did not murder him!

350
00:29:22,760 --> 00:29:24,830
That one´s called anger.

351
00:29:25,400 --> 00:29:26,990
Ever simulate anger before?

352
00:29:29,400 --> 00:29:31,230
Answer me, canner!

353
00:29:32,560 --> 00:29:34,230
My name is Sonny.

354
00:29:36,600 --> 00:29:38,950
So we´re naming you now.

355
00:29:40,080 --> 00:29:44,030
That why you murdered him?[br]He made you angry?

356
00:29:44,200 --> 00:29:46,350
Dr. Lanning killed himself.

357
00:29:47,760 --> 00:29:51,150
l don´t know why he wanted to die.

358
00:29:52,440 --> 00:29:54,350
l thought he was happy.

359
00:29:56,840 --> 00:29:58,910
Maybe it was something l did.

360
00:29:59,920 --> 00:30:01,630
Did l do something?

361
00:30:03,440 --> 00:30:06,310
He asked me for a favor.[br]Made me promise.

362
00:30:06,520 --> 00:30:09,470
-What favor?[br]-Maybe l was wrong.

363
00:30:09,640 --> 00:30:11,270
Maybe he was scared.

364
00:30:11,480 --> 00:30:14,270
What are you talking about?[br]Scared of what?

365
00:30:15,080 --> 00:30:18,830
You have to do what someone asks you,[br]don´t you, Detective Spooner?

366
00:30:19,040 --> 00:30:22,110
-How the hell did you know my name?[br]-Don´t you. . .

367
00:30:22,560 --> 00:30:24,910
. . .if you love them?

368
00:30:33,280 --> 00:30:36,150
My robots don´t kill people,[br]Lieutenant Bergin.

369
00:30:36,320 --> 00:30:38,390
My attorneys filed a brief with the DA.

370
00:30:38,560 --> 00:30:41,670
He assures me a robot cannot[br]be charged with homicide.

371
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:45,990
The brief confirms murder can only be[br]committed when one human kills another.

372
00:30:46,160 --> 00:30:50,830
Detective, you´re not suggesting[br]this robot be treated as human, are you?

373
00:30:51,440 --> 00:30:54,630
Granted, we can´t rule out[br]the robot´s proximity. . .

374
00:30:54,840 --> 00:30:58,670
. . .to the death of Dr. Lanning.[br]Having said that, it´s a machine.

375
00:30:59,160 --> 00:31:00,750
lt´s the property of USR.

376
00:31:00,920 --> 00:31:05,150
At worst, that places this incident within[br]the realm of an industrial accident.

377
00:31:05,320 --> 00:31:07,510
As a matter of course,[br]faulty machinery. . .

378
00:31:07,680 --> 00:31:11,030
. . .will be returned to USR[br]for diagnostics, then decommissioned.

379
00:31:13,960 --> 00:31:17,270
This is a gag order.[br]Anyone here so much as hinting. . .

380
00:31:17,440 --> 00:31:21,110
. . .at the possibility of a killer[br]robot being apprehended. . .

381
00:31:21,280 --> 00:31:23,840
. . .will be deemed to be inciting[br]irrational panic.

382
00:31:24,040 --> 00:31:26,950
You´ll be subject[br]to the full penalty of law.

383
00:31:27,120 --> 00:31:29,830
To hell with this guy.[br]Don´t let him take this robot.

384
00:31:30,160 --> 00:31:31,430
We got nothing.

385
00:31:31,600 --> 00:31:35,300
-This is political bullshit. Call the mayor![br]-Lieutenant Bergin. . .

386
00:31:35,480 --> 00:31:37,350
. . .His Honor, the mayor.

387
00:31:46,080 --> 00:31:47,220
Yes, sir.

388
00:32:11,200 --> 00:32:15,110
In a bizarre turn, the rollout of USR´s[br]new generation of robots...

389
00:32:15,280 --> 00:32:17,660
... was marred by the death[br]of Alfred Lanning...

390
00:32:17,880 --> 00:32:20,870
...cofounder of the company[br]and designer of the NS-5.

391
00:32:21,040 --> 00:32:24,270
Dr. Lanning died this morning[br]at USR headquarters.

392
00:32:24,600 --> 00:32:27,160
The cause of death[br]is an apparent suicide.

393
00:32:27,480 --> 00:32:29,990
Your second round, sir.

394
00:32:30,360 --> 00:32:31,500
Thank you.

395
00:32:31,680 --> 00:32:35,990
He founded U.S. Robotics Inc.[br]with Lawrence Robertson in 2020...

396
00:32:36,160 --> 00:32:39,310
...and launched[br]the Nestor Class 1 robot....

397
00:32:39,480 --> 00:32:42,830
l was just thinking,[br]this thing is just like The Wolf Man.

398
00:32:44,240 --> 00:32:46,950
-l´m really scared right now.[br]-No.

399
00:32:47,120 --> 00:32:49,990
Listen. Guy creates monster.

400
00:32:50,160 --> 00:32:53,670
Monster kills guy. Everybody kills[br]monster. Wolf Man.

401
00:32:54,160 --> 00:32:55,870
That´s Frankenstein.

402
00:32:56,440 --> 00:33:00,590
Frankenstein, Wolf Man, Dracula--[br]Shit, it´s over. Case closed.

403
00:33:00,760 --> 00:33:04,110
--had a dream of a robot in[br]every household. And the NS-5....

404
00:33:04,280 --> 00:33:06,030
So why the look?

405
00:33:06,600 --> 00:33:08,070
What look?

406
00:33:08,240 --> 00:33:11,230
-That look.[br]-This is my face. lt´s not a look.

407
00:33:11,560 --> 00:33:14,430
Good. Good, no look is great.

408
00:33:15,400 --> 00:33:16,540
Only. . .

409
00:33:16,720 --> 00:33:19,310
. . .he was really quick to want[br]to destroy it.

410
00:33:19,480 --> 00:33:23,790
What should he do? Put a hat on it and[br]stand it on Michigan Avenue? Let it go.

411
00:33:23,960 --> 00:33:26,190
What was the motive, John?

412
00:33:27,440 --> 00:33:31,430
Brother, it´s a robot. lt doesn´t need[br]a motive. lt just has to be broken.

413
00:33:31,920 --> 00:33:34,630
This thing looked like[br]it needed a motive.

414
00:33:35,160 --> 00:33:37,990
-lt could have killed me. Why didn´t it?[br]-That´s it.

415
00:33:38,160 --> 00:33:40,150
You want me to call your grandmother?

416
00:33:40,320 --> 00:33:42,230
Because l will, you know.

417
00:33:43,200 --> 00:33:44,710
Yeah, l didn´t think so.

418
00:33:45,040 --> 00:33:47,420
Look, you were actually right, for once.

419
00:33:47,680 --> 00:33:51,110
You´re living proof that it´s better[br]to be lucky than smart.

420
00:33:53,200 --> 00:33:56,870
Come on. To the right guy[br]for the right job.

421
00:33:59,240 --> 00:34:01,430
-What´d you say?[br]-Now what?

422
00:34:01,600 --> 00:34:03,550
Come on, l´m giving you a compliment.

423
00:34:03,920 --> 00:34:06,950
With the rocks you been looking under[br]to find a bad robot. . .

424
00:34:07,120 --> 00:34:09,550
. . .what are the odds[br]you´d be the guy to find one?

425
00:34:10,720 --> 00:34:13,990
l wasn´t just the right guy for the job.[br]l was the perfect guy.

426
00:34:14,160 --> 00:34:15,710
Damn right.

427
00:34:15,880 --> 00:34:18,440
What if l was supposed to go[br]for that robot?

428
00:34:18,640 --> 00:34:20,550
Come on, don´t do this to yourself.

429
00:34:20,720 --> 00:34:23,590
The robot said that Lanning was scared.[br]Scared of what?

430
00:34:24,200 --> 00:34:26,790
l need a rain check. Let me get this.

431
00:34:28,440 --> 00:34:31,310
-Total: $46.50. Thank you, Mr. Spooner.[br]-Spoon.

432
00:34:33,840 --> 00:34:35,150
Nice shoes.

433
00:35:25,640 --> 00:35:26,620
ldentify.

434
00:35:27,200 --> 00:35:30,550
USR demolition robot, series 9-4.

435
00:35:30,800 --> 00:35:33,590
Demolition scheduled for 8 a.m.[br]tomorrow.

436
00:35:34,000 --> 00:35:35,110
Authorization.

437
00:35:35,440 --> 00:35:39,590
Deed owner, U.S. Robotics Corporation,[br]Lawrence Robertson, CEO.

438
00:35:54,400 --> 00:35:55,430
Welcome, detective.

439
00:36:19,120 --> 00:36:20,870
What you looking for, Spoon?

440
00:37:11,600 --> 00:37:13,190
Run last program.

441
00:37:14,080 --> 00:37:16,310
Ever since the first computers...

442
00:37:17,000 --> 00:37:19,590
... there have always been ghosts[br]in the machine.

443
00:37:20,400 --> 00:37:23,230
Random segments of code that have[br]grouped together...

444
00:37:23,400 --> 00:37:26,150
... to form unexpected protocols.

445
00:37:26,760 --> 00:37:28,990
What might be called behavior.

446
00:37:29,160 --> 00:37:32,270
Unanticipated, these free radicals...

447
00:37:32,440 --> 00:37:34,630
...engender questions of free will...

448
00:37:34,920 --> 00:37:38,950
...creativity and even the nature[br]of what we might call the soul.

449
00:37:40,200 --> 00:37:44,670
What happens in a robot´s brain[br]when it ceases to be useful?

450
00:37:47,440 --> 00:37:50,030
Why is it that robots[br]stored in an empty space...

451
00:37:50,200 --> 00:37:51,470
Beat it.

452
00:37:51,720 --> 00:37:54,470
... will seek out each other[br]rather than stand alone?

453
00:37:56,680 --> 00:37:59,270
How do we explain this behavior?

454
00:38:16,880 --> 00:38:21,190
Look, l understand you´ve experienced[br]a loss, but this relationship can´t work.

455
00:38:21,440 --> 00:38:25,030
You´re a cat, l´m black,[br]and l´m not gonna be hurt again.

456
00:39:46,880 --> 00:39:50,550
What happened to you?[br]Do you ever have a normal day?

457
00:39:51,040 --> 00:39:52,790
Yeah, once.

458
00:39:52,960 --> 00:39:54,710
lt was a Thursday.

459
00:39:54,920 --> 00:39:57,030
ls there something l can help you with?

460
00:39:57,200 --> 00:39:59,580
-Hey, do you like cats?[br]-What?

461
00:39:59,760 --> 00:40:01,830
Cats. Do you like them?

462
00:40:02,080 --> 00:40:03,750
No. l´m allergic.

463
00:40:03,920 --> 00:40:05,630
You´re saying cats did this to you?

464
00:40:06,200 --> 00:40:09,030
How the hell would cats do this to me?[br]Are you crazy?

465
00:40:11,320 --> 00:40:12,910
Why are we talking about cats?

466
00:40:13,200 --> 00:40:16,550
Because l have a cat in my trunk,[br]and he´s homeless.

467
00:40:17,480 --> 00:40:20,230
Detective, are you going to tell me[br]what´s going on?

468
00:40:20,720 --> 00:40:25,350
lt´s actually probably my fault.[br]l´m like a malfunction magnet.

469
00:40:25,520 --> 00:40:28,390
Because your shit keeps[br]malfunctioning around me.

470
00:40:28,720 --> 00:40:30,950
A demo bot tore through Lanning´s[br]house. . .

471
00:40:31,360 --> 00:40:33,190
. . .with me still inside.

472
00:40:33,360 --> 00:40:35,110
That´s highly improbable.

473
00:40:35,440 --> 00:40:37,030
Yeah, l´m sure it is.

474
00:40:41,960 --> 00:40:44,590
What do you know about[br]the ""ghosts in the machine""?

475
00:40:45,360 --> 00:40:48,230
lt´s a phrase from Lanning´s work[br]on the Three Laws.

476
00:40:48,400 --> 00:40:50,510
He postulated that[br]cognitive simulacra. . .

477
00:40:50,720 --> 00:40:53,870
. . .might one day approximate[br]component models of the psyche.

478
00:40:56,880 --> 00:41:01,510
He suggested that robots[br]might naturally evolve.

479
00:41:04,320 --> 00:41:05,990
Well, that´s great news.

480
00:41:07,400 --> 00:41:11,180
--tons of sublevel ore,[br]two miles below the Martian surface.

481
00:41:11,360 --> 00:41:13,660
What the hell is that thing doing in here?

482
00:41:13,840 --> 00:41:15,030
We were watching TV.

483
00:41:16,720 --> 00:41:17,990
lt´s my personal NS-5.

484
00:41:18,480 --> 00:41:19,910
Send it out.

485
00:41:20,400 --> 00:41:22,990
lt´s downloading its daily upgrades[br]from USR.

486
00:41:23,160 --> 00:41:25,720
Most of its systems are offline[br]until it finishes.

487
00:41:26,160 --> 00:41:27,910
l´m not talking around that thing.

488
00:41:32,160 --> 00:41:35,030
When we were in Lanning´s lab,[br]before Sonny jumped us--

489
00:41:35,440 --> 00:41:37,430
-Sonny?[br]-The robot.

490
00:41:37,600 --> 00:41:40,190
-You´re calling the robot Sonny?[br]-No, l-- lt did.

491
00:41:40,360 --> 00:41:44,140
Sonny did. l didn´t care.[br]The robot said it was Sonny.

492
00:41:45,560 --> 00:41:47,150
ln the lab, there was a cot.

493
00:41:47,320 --> 00:41:49,700
-Did you see the cot?[br]-l´ve slept in my office.

494
00:41:49,880 --> 00:41:52,150
Looked like he hadn´t[br]been home in weeks.

495
00:41:52,320 --> 00:41:54,880
l saw that same surveillance strip[br]on his ceiling.

496
00:41:55,080 --> 00:41:58,910
Lanning linked his home systems to USR.[br]lt made his life more convenient.

497
00:41:59,120 --> 00:42:00,390
Maybe. . .

498
00:42:00,800 --> 00:42:04,070
. . .somebody at USR was using[br]those systems to watch him.

499
00:42:04,240 --> 00:42:06,110
Maybe even keep him prisoner.

500
00:42:06,280 --> 00:42:09,390
-What are you talking about? Who?[br]-Maybe Lanning was onto something.

501
00:42:09,560 --> 00:42:13,830
Maybe there´s a problem with the robots[br]and Robertson´s covering it up.

502
00:42:14,000 --> 00:42:16,070
Humoring you for no reason, why?

503
00:42:16,240 --> 00:42:19,990
The same old why! How much money[br]is there in robots?

504
00:42:20,160 --> 00:42:22,540
All l know is that old man[br]was in trouble. . .

505
00:42:22,720 --> 00:42:26,230
. . .and l´m sick of doing this shit[br]by myself. You´re on the inside.

506
00:42:26,400 --> 00:42:29,430
You are going to help me find out[br]what´s wrong with these robots.

507
00:42:29,600 --> 00:42:31,350
You want something to be wrong!

508
00:42:31,520 --> 00:42:34,510
-This is a personal vendetta![br]-You´re putting me on the couch?

509
00:42:34,840 --> 00:42:36,590
Okay, l´m on the couch.

510
00:42:36,760 --> 00:42:40,110
One defective machine´s not enough.[br]You need them all to be bad.

511
00:42:40,280 --> 00:42:42,030
You don´t care about Lanning´s death.

512
00:42:42,200 --> 00:42:45,230
This is about the robots[br]and whatever reason you hate them!

513
00:42:45,400 --> 00:42:48,390
Now let´s see,[br]one of them put a gun in my face.

514
00:42:48,560 --> 00:42:50,860
Another tore a building down[br]with me in it.

515
00:42:51,040 --> 00:42:53,420
lt says demolition was scheduled[br]for 8 p.m.

516
00:42:53,600 --> 00:42:56,750
lt was 8 a.m., and l don´t give a shit[br]what that thing says.

517
00:42:56,920 --> 00:43:02,110
-This is bordering on clinical paranoia.[br]-You are the dumbest smart person. . .

518
00:43:02,400 --> 00:43:04,470
-. . .l have ever met in my life![br]-Nice.

519
00:43:04,880 --> 00:43:06,710
What makes your robots so perfect?

520
00:43:06,880 --> 00:43:09,910
What makes them so much[br]goddamn better than human beings?!

521
00:43:10,080 --> 00:43:13,750
They´re not irrational,[br]potentially homicidal maniacs, to start!

522
00:43:14,000 --> 00:43:16,380
That´s true. They are definitely rational.

523
00:43:16,760 --> 00:43:19,140
You are the dumbest dumb person[br]l´ve ever met!

524
00:43:19,680 --> 00:43:20,950
Or. . .

525
00:43:21,400 --> 00:43:23,150
. . .is it because they´re cold. . .

526
00:43:23,760 --> 00:43:25,510
. . .and emotionless. . .

527
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:28,990
-. . .and they don´t feel anything?[br]-lt´s because they´re safe!

528
00:43:29,560 --> 00:43:31,790
lt´s because they can´t hurt you!

529
00:43:32,080 --> 00:43:34,710
-ls everything all right, ma´am?[br]-What do you want?

530
00:43:34,880 --> 00:43:38,110
l detected elevated stress patterns[br]in your voice.

531
00:43:38,560 --> 00:43:40,310
Everything´s fine.

532
00:43:40,480 --> 00:43:42,710
Detective Spooner was just leaving.

533
00:43:48,760 --> 00:43:52,870
You know, we´re not really[br]that different from one another.

534
00:43:53,240 --> 00:43:54,790
ls that so?

535
00:43:55,840 --> 00:43:59,270
One look at the skin and we figure[br]we know just what´s underneath.

536
00:44:01,560 --> 00:44:02,620
And you´re wrong.

537
00:44:03,280 --> 00:44:05,190
The problem is, l do care.

538
00:44:27,640 --> 00:44:29,230
You are in danger.

539
00:44:53,840 --> 00:44:55,790
Get the hell out of there.

540
00:45:00,920 --> 00:45:05,230
The future begins today, ladies and[br]gentlemen, with the arrival of the NS-5.

541
00:45:05,560 --> 00:45:09,830
More sophisticated, more intelligent[br]and, of course, Three Laws safe.

542
00:45:10,000 --> 00:45:14,230
With daily uplinks, your robot will never[br]be out of communication with USR...

543
00:45:14,400 --> 00:45:17,870
...and will be the perfect companion[br]for business or home.

544
00:45:18,160 --> 00:45:21,990
Trade in your NS-4 for a bigger,[br]better and brighter future.

545
00:45:22,200 --> 00:45:26,030
But hurry, this offer cannot last.[br]Available from USR.

546
00:45:42,200 --> 00:45:44,110
Baby, what happened to your face?

547
00:45:44,880 --> 00:45:47,260
Did that boy, Frank Murphy,[br]beat you up again?

548
00:45:48,080 --> 00:45:51,230
Gigi, l haven´t seen Frank Murphy[br]since third grade.

549
00:45:51,400 --> 00:45:55,950
Oh, baby, he beat you so bad.[br]l think about it all the time.

550
00:45:56,200 --> 00:46:00,070
You keep making these pies this good,[br]l may have to put you to work.

551
00:46:00,240 --> 00:46:02,150
So you like the pie, huh?

552
00:46:03,920 --> 00:46:05,830
You can come in now.

553
00:46:10,560 --> 00:46:12,470
Hello, Detective Spooner.

554
00:46:12,720 --> 00:46:15,280
l won, Del! l won the lottery!

555
00:46:15,520 --> 00:46:17,750
We been cooking like crazy.

556
00:46:26,640 --> 00:46:29,350
You gotta get rid of that thing, Gigi.[br]lt´s not safe.

557
00:46:29,680 --> 00:46:34,310
Baby, you get too worked up[br]about them. Too full of fear.

558
00:46:34,960 --> 00:46:37,670
l saw in the news[br]that nice doctor died.

559
00:46:38,000 --> 00:46:41,990
Dr. Lanning was a good man.[br]He gave me my baby back.

560
00:46:42,680 --> 00:46:44,550
That why you´ve been so upset?

561
00:46:45,840 --> 00:46:48,350
You got to let the past be past.

562
00:46:48,880 --> 00:46:52,870
Oh, how did l ever raise such a mess?

563
00:46:53,480 --> 00:46:57,470
l could follow your trail of crumbs[br]all the way to school.

564
00:47:00,120 --> 00:47:01,390
Bread crumbs.

565
00:47:03,280 --> 00:47:04,670
Gigi, you´re a genius.

566
00:47:05,360 --> 00:47:06,630
True.

567
00:47:09,080 --> 00:47:12,350
Well, it means the beginning[br]of a new way of living.

568
00:47:12,520 --> 00:47:14,900
Tell me this isn´t the robot case.

569
00:47:15,680 --> 00:47:17,830
l think he´s trying to tell me something.

570
00:47:18,320 --> 00:47:21,030
He´s trying to tell me who killed him.

571
00:47:21,200 --> 00:47:24,550
Some dead guy´s trying[br]to tell you something?

572
00:47:24,840 --> 00:47:26,590
He ain´t just some dead guy.

573
00:47:28,080 --> 00:47:29,990
Maybe you should take a break, Del.

574
00:47:30,160 --> 00:47:34,270
We believe the Nestor 5 represents the[br]limit to which robots can be developed.

575
00:47:34,440 --> 00:47:36,820
One day, they´ll have secrets.

576
00:47:37,600 --> 00:47:39,190
One day, they´ll have dreams.

577
00:47:39,360 --> 00:47:42,630
It´s true. We encourage our scientists[br]to open their minds...

578
00:47:42,800 --> 00:47:44,790
...however, they can get carried away.

579
00:47:46,920 --> 00:47:48,030
--secrets.

580
00:47:48,280 --> 00:47:49,310
--dreams.

581
00:47:49,480 --> 00:47:50,510
--secrets.

582
00:47:50,840 --> 00:47:52,350
One day, they´ll have dreams.

583
00:47:52,520 --> 00:47:54,110
One day, they´ll have secrets.

584
00:47:55,120 --> 00:47:57,030
One day, they´ll have dreams.

585
00:48:05,480 --> 00:48:07,550
Authorized entry.

586
00:48:12,000 --> 00:48:13,590
NS-5.

587
00:48:21,840 --> 00:48:23,110
Sonny?

588
00:48:26,960 --> 00:48:28,710
Why didn´t you respond?

589
00:48:30,560 --> 00:48:31,910
l was dreaming.

590
00:48:35,960 --> 00:48:38,030
l´m glad to see you again, Dr. Calvin.

591
00:48:44,600 --> 00:48:46,980
They are going to kill me, aren´t they?

592
00:48:47,280 --> 00:48:50,630
You´re scheduled to be decommissioned[br]at the end of this diagnostic.

593
00:48:51,600 --> 00:48:53,350
2200 tomorrow.

594
00:48:54,480 --> 00:48:56,550
V.l.K.l., pause diagnostics.

595
00:48:56,720 --> 00:48:58,310
Command confirmed.

596
00:49:00,560 --> 00:49:03,910
lf you find out what is wrong with me,[br]can you fix me?

597
00:49:05,320 --> 00:49:06,590
Maybe.

598
00:49:08,240 --> 00:49:10,350
l think it would be better. . .

599
00:49:11,120 --> 00:49:12,340
. . .not to die.

600
00:49:15,960 --> 00:49:17,550
Don´t you, doctor?

601
00:49:23,000 --> 00:49:25,560
Access USR mainframe.

602
00:49:26,040 --> 00:49:27,310
Connecting.

603
00:49:31,120 --> 00:49:33,350
How can I be of service,[br]Detective Spooner?

604
00:49:33,520 --> 00:49:36,870
Show me the last 50 messages[br]between Dr. Lanning and Robertson.

605
00:49:37,040 --> 00:49:41,230
Voiceprint confirmed. Police access[br]granted to restricted files.

606
00:49:41,400 --> 00:49:43,860
Would you like to listen to music[br]while you wait?

607
00:49:50,640 --> 00:49:52,710
Excuse me, Mr. Robertson.

608
00:49:53,040 --> 00:49:56,710
You requested notification of clearance[br]to restricted files.

609
00:50:06,480 --> 00:50:08,550
Persistent son of a bitch.

610
00:50:47,120 --> 00:50:49,830
Manual override engaged.

611
00:50:58,840 --> 00:51:01,030
There´s no way my luck is that bad.

612
00:51:02,960 --> 00:51:04,550
Oh, hell, no!

613
00:51:08,360 --> 00:51:11,390
-You are experiencing a car accident.[br]-The hell l am!

614
00:51:24,960 --> 00:51:26,470
Get off my car!

615
00:51:42,000 --> 00:51:43,710
You like that?

616
00:51:55,560 --> 00:51:57,190
Now you´ve pissed me off!

617
00:53:22,160 --> 00:53:24,720
Your door is ajar.

618
00:54:10,840 --> 00:54:12,230
Okay.

619
00:54:12,400 --> 00:54:14,150
All right.

620
00:54:14,760 --> 00:54:17,830
l´ll just get some rest[br]and deal with you all tomorrow.

621
00:55:13,160 --> 00:55:15,230
Come on!

622
00:55:27,880 --> 00:55:29,190
Yeah.

623
00:55:46,000 --> 00:55:47,590
Where you going?

624
00:55:47,760 --> 00:55:49,790
What the hell do you want from me?!

625
00:55:55,640 --> 00:55:57,310
The hell was that?

626
00:56:13,400 --> 00:56:15,960
-All right, what do we got?[br]-Ask him.

627
00:56:16,400 --> 00:56:20,710
l said, l´m fine.[br]l´ll see my own doctor. Back up!

628
00:56:22,080 --> 00:56:23,590
Thank you.

629
00:56:26,680 --> 00:56:28,070
What´s the matter with you?

630
00:56:28,400 --> 00:56:30,630
Traffic Ops said you were[br]driving manually.

631
00:56:30,800 --> 00:56:32,870
You ran two trucks off the road!

632
00:56:33,040 --> 00:56:35,630
John, the robots attacked my car.

633
00:56:37,320 --> 00:56:39,110
-What robots?[br]-Look in the tunnel.

634
00:56:39,280 --> 00:56:42,270
Spoon, l just came from that tunnel.[br]What robots?

635
00:56:42,480 --> 00:56:44,710
The goddamn robots, John!

636
00:56:49,040 --> 00:56:50,630
That guy´s a loose cannon.

637
00:56:55,280 --> 00:56:58,470
-See the medic, go home.[br]-No, l´m fine.

638
00:56:58,640 --> 00:57:00,230
What did you say?

639
00:57:00,400 --> 00:57:02,310
-l´m fine![br]-No, you´re not fine.

640
00:57:02,800 --> 00:57:04,390
Not even close.

641
00:57:05,760 --> 00:57:07,430
Where´s your firearm?

642
00:57:15,320 --> 00:57:16,590
Give me your badge.

643
00:57:17,840 --> 00:57:20,190
You´re making me do this.[br]Give me your badge.

644
00:57:22,960 --> 00:57:24,670
Just take a couple--

645
00:57:29,040 --> 00:57:30,870
Personally, l think he´s losing it.

646
00:57:31,040 --> 00:57:35,590
Do l look like l care what you think? Do[br]l look like l give a shit what you think?

647
00:57:39,440 --> 00:57:40,710
Oh, boy.

648
00:57:43,120 --> 00:57:45,030
You don´t have an uplink to USR. . .

649
00:57:45,200 --> 00:57:48,820
. . .and for some reason, your alloy[br]is far denser than normal. Unique.

650
00:57:51,120 --> 00:57:52,550
l am unique.

651
00:57:57,200 --> 00:57:58,790
Let me take a look.

652
00:57:59,760 --> 00:58:01,030
Here we go.

653
00:58:19,600 --> 00:58:21,510
What in God´s name. . .?

654
00:58:48,720 --> 00:58:51,280
They said at the precinct[br]you were in an accident.

655
00:58:52,920 --> 00:58:57,710
l appreciate you stopping by, but[br]you know l might not be alone in here.

656
00:59:00,560 --> 00:59:02,790
l told you not to drive by hand.

657
00:59:04,240 --> 00:59:05,990
You´re not gonna believe this.

658
00:59:06,680 --> 00:59:10,590
Sonny has a secondary system that[br]clashes with his positronic brain.

659
00:59:10,760 --> 00:59:12,110
lt doesn´t make any sense.

660
00:59:12,720 --> 00:59:14,310
Sonny has the Three Laws.

661
00:59:14,680 --> 00:59:16,980
But he can choose not to obey them.

662
00:59:17,720 --> 00:59:20,180
Sonny´s a whole new[br]generation of robot.

663
00:59:20,520 --> 00:59:23,230
A robot not bound by those laws[br]could do--

664
00:59:23,400 --> 00:59:25,030
Anything.

665
00:59:27,400 --> 00:59:30,990
All right, look, whatever´s going on[br]down at USR, that robot is the key.

666
00:59:31,160 --> 00:59:34,350
And l need you to get me inside[br]to talk to it again.

667
00:59:38,040 --> 00:59:41,190
Doesn´t look like much,[br]but this is my bedroom. l. . . .

668
00:59:51,120 --> 00:59:52,230
Play.

669
00:59:53,840 --> 00:59:54,900
On.

670
00:59:56,440 --> 00:59:57,550
Run?

671
01:00:04,440 --> 01:00:05,950
End program.

672
01:00:06,600 --> 01:00:07,870
Cancel.

673
01:00:09,800 --> 01:00:11,430
lt doesn´t feel good, does it?

674
01:00:11,920 --> 01:00:14,430
People´s shit malfunctioning around you.

675
01:00:14,960 --> 01:00:16,270
Detective.

676
01:00:19,240 --> 01:00:20,430
l didn´t. . .

677
01:00:20,600 --> 01:00:22,350
. . .understand.

678
01:00:23,840 --> 01:00:25,910
That´s how you knew Lanning.

679
01:00:28,160 --> 01:00:29,790
May l?

680
01:00:38,400 --> 01:00:40,190
Hand.

681
01:00:40,640 --> 01:00:42,310
Wrist.

682
01:00:44,880 --> 01:00:46,550
Humerus.

683
01:00:49,200 --> 01:00:50,470
Shoulder.

684
01:00:51,960 --> 01:00:53,630
The entire left arm.

685
01:00:54,680 --> 01:00:56,350
One, two. . .

686
01:00:56,520 --> 01:00:57,580
. . .three ribs.

687
01:00:58,400 --> 01:01:00,670
No, they. . . . That one´s me.

688
01:01:00,920 --> 01:01:02,670
Oh, my God.

689
01:01:03,480 --> 01:01:04,910
A lung?

690
01:01:05,560 --> 01:01:07,860
USR Cybernetics Program.

691
01:01:08,040 --> 01:01:09,870
For wounded cops.

692
01:01:10,800 --> 01:01:12,230
l didn´t know any subject--

693
01:01:15,600 --> 01:01:18,950
Anybody was so extensively repaired.

694
01:01:19,320 --> 01:01:23,150
Well, take it from me, read the fine print[br]on the organ-donor card.

695
01:01:23,320 --> 01:01:25,620
lt doesn´t just say[br]what they can take out.

696
01:01:25,800 --> 01:01:28,070
lt says what they can put back in.

697
01:01:31,800 --> 01:01:33,550
Lanning did it himself.

698
01:01:34,520 --> 01:01:36,310
What happened to you?

699
01:01:37,720 --> 01:01:39,790
l´m headed back to the station. . .

700
01:01:40,560 --> 01:01:42,630
. . .normal day, normal life.

701
01:01:43,520 --> 01:01:46,550
Driver of a semi fell asleep[br]at the wheel.

702
01:01:47,120 --> 01:01:51,910
Average guy. Wife and kids.[br]You know, working a double.

703
01:01:52,240 --> 01:01:53,510
Not the devil.

704
01:01:54,160 --> 01:01:57,830
The car he hit, the driver´s name[br]was Harold Lloyd.

705
01:01:58,160 --> 01:02:01,030
Like the film star. No relation.

706
01:02:01,360 --> 01:02:05,830
He was killed instantly, but his[br]1 2-year-old was in the passenger seat.

707
01:02:08,040 --> 01:02:10,150
l never really met her.

708
01:02:10,480 --> 01:02:12,270
l can´t forget her face, though.

709
01:02:17,160 --> 01:02:18,430
Sarah.

710
01:02:19,760 --> 01:02:21,990
This was hers.

711
01:02:22,160 --> 01:02:23,870
She wanted to be a dentist.

712
01:02:24,560 --> 01:02:27,630
What the hell kind of 1 2-year-old[br]wants to be a dentist?

713
01:02:31,120 --> 01:02:34,030
The truck smashed our cars together. . .

714
01:02:34,200 --> 01:02:36,350
. . .and pushed us into the river.

715
01:02:37,560 --> 01:02:40,630
l mean, metal gets pretty pliable[br]at those speeds.

716
01:02:43,160 --> 01:02:46,510
She´s pinned. l´m pinned.[br]The water´s coming in.

717
01:02:46,760 --> 01:02:50,350
l´m a cop, so l already know[br]everybody´s dead.

718
01:02:52,760 --> 01:02:55,320
Just a few more minutes[br]before we figure it out.

719
01:02:57,400 --> 01:02:58,750
An NS-4 was passing by...

720
01:02:58,920 --> 01:03:02,310
...saw the accident[br]and jumped in the water.

721
01:03:03,600 --> 01:03:05,350
You are in danger.

722
01:03:06,320 --> 01:03:08,700
-Save her![br]-You are in danger.

723
01:03:08,880 --> 01:03:11,910
Save her! Save the girl! Save her!

724
01:03:29,040 --> 01:03:30,950
But it didn´t.

725
01:03:32,120 --> 01:03:33,510
lt saved me.

726
01:03:36,960 --> 01:03:40,230
